window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["mlops"]=[{"id":"mlops-f0001","card":"q-mlops-001-1-scope-big-picture","sec":"mlops-s002","q":"In MLOps, which activities fall on the \"build\" side and which on the \"run\" side?","a":"<p>Build side: requirements, data and training pipelines, data governance, experiment tracking, integrations and model management. Run side: infrastructure, deployment, serving, monitoring and responsible AI. Core ML has the same split: feature engineering, training, testing and packaging are build, while deployment and inference are run. Splitting this way lets different teams own each side with their own tooling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0002","card":"q-mlops-001-1-scope-big-picture","sec":"mlops-s002","q":"Why should you re-evaluate MLOps tooling at the start of each project instead of standardizing once?","a":"<p>The MLOps tool market changes fast. Tools appear, merge and get deprecated, and the cloud providers (AWS, GCP, Azure) each ship their own MLOps stack. A choice made two years ago may not be the best or supported option today. The trade-off is re-evaluation effort versus lock-in to an outdated tool.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0003","card":"q-mlops-002-ml-life-cycle","sec":"mlops-s003","q":"Walk through the stages of the ML life cycle in order.","a":"<p>Requirements, design, data acquisition, feature engineering, model training, testing and evaluation, deployment and inference, then data collection. Production data is collected and labeled, and that becomes new training data, so the cycle goes back to data acquisition.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0004","card":"q-mlops-002-ml-life-cycle","sec":"mlops-s003","q":"Why is the ML life cycle described as cyclic and not linear?","a":"<p>A model is never finished. Production data is collected and labeled, and it becomes the training data for the next round, so the last stage feeds back into data acquisition. Retraining is the normal path, not an exception.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0005","card":"q-mlops-002-ml-life-cycle","sec":"mlops-s003","q":"Is MLOps the same thing as building the model? Explain.","a":"<p>No. MLOps is the ecosystem of processes, automation and tooling around the ML life cycle: data management, tracking, versioning, integration, deployment and monitoring. Model building (feature engineering, training, evaluation) is one stage inside it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0006","card":"q-mlops-003-why-ml-is-harder-than-software-engineeri","sec":"mlops-s004","q":"Why is managing an ML project harder than managing a normal software project?","a":"<p>Software has two artifact types: code and records (requirements, design, test plans and results). ML adds two more: data (training, production, labeled) and models (trained artifacts). Each has its own life cycle but depends on the others, so you need extra coordination, tooling, skill sets and an end-to-end workflow.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0007","card":"q-mlops-003-why-ml-is-harder-than-software-engineeri","sec":"mlops-s004","q":"How do the four ML artifacts depend on one another?","a":"<p>Requirements guide data preparation. Data plus requirements produce the model. The model is integrated into code. Running that code in production produces new data, which feeds the next training round. A change in one artifact can force changes in the others.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0008","card":"q-mlops-003-why-ml-is-harder-than-software-engineeri","sec":"mlops-s004","q":"What organizational challenges does an ML project add compared with plain software delivery?","a":"<p>Data engineers, ML engineers and product owners must collaborate, and each artifact needs specialized skills. Dependencies between artifact life cycles need careful coordination. You also need tools to govern every artifact type, not only code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0009","card":"q-mlops-004-devops-vs-mlops","sec":"mlops-s005","q":"How does MLOps differ from DevOps?","a":"<p>DevOps integrates development, testing and operations for software with agile practices and automation. MLOps extends it to ML: it adds data engineering and model development to the software life cycle and manages four artifact types (code, records, data, models). It also covers model-specific concerns such as retraining, drift monitoring and feedback of production data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0010","card":"q-mlops-004-devops-vs-mlops","sec":"mlops-s005","q":"Is MLOps a tool or a product you can buy? Explain.","a":"<p>No. MLOps is a set of best practices supported by automation and tools. Tools help but do not replace the processes and team agreements.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0011","card":"q-mlops-004-devops-vs-mlops","sec":"mlops-s005","q":"Why does DevOps have the same team own development, testing and operations?","a":"<p>A single team owning the whole flow removes hand-offs between separate teams, shortens feedback loops and makes failures in production visible to the people who wrote the code. Automation of build, test and deploy makes this practical.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0012","card":"q-mlops-004-devops-vs-mlops","sec":"mlops-s005","q":"What business benefit does MLOps claim over ad hoc model development?","a":"<p>Faster time to market through continuous model development and integration, plus reliable deployment, monitoring and feedback. Models reach production in small increments instead of one large, risky release.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0013","card":"q-mlops-005-mlops-life-cycle-software-engineering-ml","sec":"mlops-s006","q":"At the start of an ML project, why does requirements and design split into two parallel tracks?","a":"<p>The ML application has ML parts and non-ML parts. One track covers non-ML development (APIs, services, databases, UIs). The other covers data engineering and model building. They run in parallel and meet at continuous integration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0014","card":"q-mlops-005-mlops-life-cycle-software-engineering-ml","sec":"mlops-s006","q":"What does model governance do in the MLOps life cycle?","a":"<p>It tracks, evaluates and manages models under a governance framework. It receives performance, drift and bias feedback from monitoring and decides whether the model stays, is retrained or is retired. If retraining is needed it starts a new training cycle with new data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0015","card":"q-mlops-005-mlops-life-cycle-software-engineering-ml","sec":"mlops-s006","q":"Describe the feedback loops in the MLOps life cycle.","a":"<p>Monitoring sends performance, drift and bias information to model governance and back to requirements. Data capture sends production features and labels to data engineering, which builds new datasets for continuous training. Together they let the model improve after release.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0016","card":"q-mlops-005-mlops-life-cycle-software-engineering-ml","sec":"mlops-s006","q":"What is the difference between continuous integration and continuous deployment in an ML project?","a":"<p>Continuous integration combines the model with non-ML code at regular intervals (for example each sprint or week) and tests the result. Package and deliver bundles model plus code. Continuous deployment then releases approved packages to production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0017","card":"q-mlops-006-principles-of-mlops","sec":"mlops-s007","q":"Why does MLOps list \"reproducible\" as a principle, and what does it require?","a":"<p>You must be able to rebuild any artifact with the same result. That means managing both the artifact and the code, parameters and data version that produced it. Without it you cannot debug, audit or trust a model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0018","card":"q-mlops-006-principles-of-mlops","sec":"mlops-s007","q":"What is the difference between traceability and reproducibility?","a":"<p>Traceability is knowing an artifact's lineage: which data, code and parameters it came from. Reproducibility is being able to recreate it from them. Traceability is the record. Reproducibility is the proof the record is complete.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0019","card":"q-mlops-006-principles-of-mlops","sec":"mlops-s007","q":"What does the \"modular\" principle mean in an ML workflow and why does it help?","a":"<p>Pipeline parts have clear boundaries and owners, so artifacts such as data, model and application code can evolve at the same time. A problem in one module does not block the others, which also supports the resilient principle.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0020","card":"q-mlops-006-principles-of-mlops","sec":"mlops-s007","q":"How should you use the list of MLOps principles when adding a new tool or process?","a":"<p>As acceptance goals. Ask whether the new policy, process or tool makes the workflow more automated, traceable, reproducible, secure and so on. If it works against several principles, reconsider it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0021","card":"q-mlops-007-when-to-start-mlops","sec":"mlops-s008","q":"At which stage of an ML initiative should you invest in a full MLOps system, and why not earlier?","a":"<p>At the engineering stage, when requirements are concrete and a full team builds, deploys and monitors the product. During exploration and experimentation the project may be dropped, so a full MLOps system would be wasted effort. Investing too late causes inefficiency and painful integration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0022","card":"q-mlops-007-when-to-start-mlops","sec":"mlops-s008","q":"What MLOps investment makes sense in the experimentation stage?","a":"<p>Incremental. Add data and training pipelines as the experiments need them, and postpone deployment and operations tooling. The team is still choosing one use case and checking results against business expectations and cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0023","card":"q-mlops-007-when-to-start-mlops","sec":"mlops-s008","q":"What is the risk of starting MLOps too late and the risk of starting too early?","a":"<p>Too early: wasted effort if the project is dropped. Too late: manual, inconsistent processes that are slow and hard to integrate. The aim is to match investment to the stage the project has reached.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0024","card":"q-mlops-008-selecting-ml-projects","sec":"mlops-s009","q":"What criteria decide whether an organization should take on an ML project?","a":"<p>Core business value and strategic differentiation, availability of good labeled training data, an affordable technology ecosystem (algorithms, libraries, pre-trained models), budget and team size, time to market, and risk appetite. Invest only if benefits outweigh costs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0025","card":"q-mlops-008-selecting-ml-projects","sec":"mlops-s009","q":"Why is training data availability a go or no-go criterion?","a":"<p>Without enough good data, including labels for supervised problems, the model cannot reach useful quality and the project fails. Check data access, quality and labeling cost before committing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0026","card":"q-mlops-008-selecting-ml-projects","sec":"mlops-s009","q":"Why must a company accept risk before starting an ML project?","a":"<p>ML outcomes are uncertain. Performance or cost targets may not be reached even with good execution. The organization should be willing to absorb that outcome, or to stop the project.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0027","card":"q-mlops-008-selecting-ml-projects","sec":"mlops-s009","q":"Why is an understaffed ML project a bad idea?","a":"<p>It drags on and may outlive its usefulness, since competitors ship first or the business need changes. Staff to the scope or reduce the scope.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0028","card":"q-mlops-009-creating-requirements","sec":"mlops-s010","q":"What kinds of requirements does an ML application need beyond the model itself?","a":"<p>Non-ML requirements: user experience (GUI and APIs), functions (data collected, transformations, reporting), deployment, scale, security and serviceability. ML-specific requirements: problem statement, performance goals, operational goals and cost limits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0029","card":"q-mlops-009-creating-requirements","sec":"mlops-s010","q":"How should you state ML performance requirements?","a":"<p>As minimum metric values the model must achieve and maintain. Example: accuracy at least 90% on test data, latency at most 1 second at the 90th percentile. They must be measurable, a true minimum and reasonable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0030","card":"q-mlops-009-creating-requirements","sec":"mlops-s010","q":"Why include product metrics as well as model metrics in requirements?","a":"<p>A model can score well technically and still not help the business. Product metrics such as conversion rate or concurrent sessions measure business impact and capacity. Model metrics such as accuracy or latency measure the model. You need both effectiveness and efficiency in each group.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0031","card":"q-mlops-009-creating-requirements","sec":"mlops-s010","q":"How do you pick a reasonable target for a model metric?","a":"<p>Base it on known performance of existing models in the domain, other technologies on the market, customer expectations and what is achievable in the available time. Targets that are too low give no value. Targets that are too high stall the project.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0032","card":"q-mlops-009-creating-requirements","sec":"mlops-s010","q":"Why write requirements before running experiments?","a":"<p>Requirements stop experimentation from drifting. They give a clear definition of done for the model, and they provide the criteria for later promotion and benchmarking decisions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0033","card":"q-mlops-010-designing-the-ml-workflow","sec":"mlops-s011","q":"What properties should a well-designed ML workflow have?","a":"<p>Traceability of data, models and processing steps, repeatability, automation, access controls, flexibility (plug-and-play parts), defined ownership of each part, and autoscaling with data, models and teams.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0034","card":"q-mlops-010-designing-the-ml-workflow","sec":"mlops-s011","q":"What does \"data as code\" and \"model as code\" mean?","a":"<p>The steps that create data or a model are written as versioned code and pipelines, with no manual edits. You can rebuild from scratch and get the same result. It is the basis of repeatability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0035","card":"q-mlops-010-designing-the-ml-workflow","sec":"mlops-s011","q":"Why decouple the data, model, product and deployment sub-pipelines?","a":"<p>Decoupling lets each evolve independently, progress in parallel and be owned by its own sub-team. With tight coupling, a problem in one part blocks everyone. The cost is the need for clear interfaces between pipelines.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0036","card":"q-mlops-010-designing-the-ml-workflow","sec":"mlops-s011","q":"Why does an ML workflow need access controls on artifacts?","a":"<p>To define who can create, promote, use or discard data and models. This protects sensitive data, prevents unreviewed models from reaching production and supports audits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0037","card":"q-mlops-011-assembling-the-team","sec":"mlops-s012","q":"Roughly how should an ML product team be composed, and what is the common misconception?","a":"<p>A rough split: data engineers 30%, data scientists 20%, software engineers 20%, ML engineers 20%, operations engineers 10%. The misconception is that a couple of data scientists is enough. They are only about a fifth of the team.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0038","card":"q-mlops-011-assembling-the-team","sec":"mlops-s012","q":"What does an ML engineer do that a data scientist does not?","a":"<p>A data scientist builds and evaluates models. An ML engineer productizes them: scaling, packaging, integrations and pipelines so the model runs reliably in a product. Software engineers build the wrapper services and APIs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0039","card":"q-mlops-011-assembling-the-team","sec":"mlops-s012","q":"How should you grow an ML team if budget is limited?","a":"<p>Start with data engineering and data science. Add the other roles after the model proves viable. In a small team (about 3 people) hire people who can wear several hats, and add domain experts and integration leads.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0040","card":"q-mlops-011-assembling-the-team","sec":"mlops-s012","q":"How do you protect an ML project from attrition of scarce ML specialists?","a":"<p>Cross-train team members and document pipelines, decisions and models, so knowledge is not held by one person.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0041","card":"q-mlops-012-choosing-tools","sec":"mlops-s013","q":"What should you consider when choosing MLOps tools?","a":"<p>Fit to the use case (classical ML, NLP and vision differ), trial or free versions, the vendor roadmap and enterprise support, programmability and extensibility, and the current ecosystem at implementation time. Also keep the tool count low, since each tool needs administration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0042","card":"q-mlops-012-choosing-tools","sec":"mlops-s013","q":"Why decouple tools from the main ML pipeline?","a":"<p>The tool market is immature and changing, with no dominant vendor and mostly function-specific tools. If a tool is decoupled you can swap it without rewriting the pipeline, which limits lock-in.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0043","card":"q-mlops-012-choosing-tools","sec":"mlops-s013","q":"Open source MLflow versus a commercial tool such as Weights &amp; Biases versus a cloud provider's service: how do you choose?","a":"<p>Open source gives control and no license cost but you run and maintain it. Commercial tools give hosted features and support at a price. Cloud services integrate well with that cloud but tie you to it. Choose by team skills, budget, support needs and portability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0044","card":"q-mlops-013-managed-data-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s014","q":"Why should you invest in a managed data pipeline early in an ML project?","a":"<p>Data issues are the most common blocker for data scientists. A managed pipeline delivers clean, consistent, traceable features, so scientists spend time on modeling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0045","card":"q-mlops-013-managed-data-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s014","q":"What does it mean to treat a data pipeline as production code?","a":"<p>Use an agile engineering life cycle, version control, separate dev, test and prod environments with promotion policies, deployment pipelines, logging, monitoring and audits. Avoid ad hoc scripts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0046","card":"q-mlops-013-managed-data-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s014","q":"How do you make feature store contents reproducible?","a":"<p>Keep raw data, and implement every transformation as version-controlled code with no ad hoc manipulation. Reprocessing the raw data with that code version then rebuilds the same features.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0047","card":"q-mlops-013-managed-data-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s014","q":"What automation should a managed data pipeline include?","a":"<p>Triggering on new data arrival or a schedule, automatic rollback on errors, automatic reprocessing, and reporting and analysis of all errors. This keeps the feature store consistent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0048","card":"q-mlops-013-managed-data-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s014","q":"Describe the flow of data through an ML data pipeline.","a":"<p>Raw data (batch or streaming) is cleansed, filtered and validated, then transformed and enriched, then stored in the feature store. From there it feeds model training. Production data comes back as new data and the cycle repeats.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0049","card":"q-mlops-013-managed-data-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s014","q":"What is data lineage and why does the pipeline record it?","a":"<p>Lineage is the history of a dataset: its origin, timing, processing steps and exceptions. It lets you trace a bad prediction to the data behind it and explain results in audits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0050","card":"q-mlops-014-automated-data-validation","sec":"mlops-s015","q":"Why validate new data automatically after a model is deployed?","a":"<p>Data keeps arriving, and it may deviate from the patterns of the training set. Detecting deviations before they reach the model prevents quality loss. Data produced by machines can also be wrong, so even automated feeds need checks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0051","card":"q-mlops-014-automated-data-validation","sec":"mlops-s015","q":"What kinds of checks does automated data validation run?","a":"<p>Feature checks (missing values, formats, ranges), distribution checks (mean, standard deviation, quartiles, class frequencies against a baseline), out-of-distribution checks (outliers, unseen categories) and correlation checks (feature-target and feature-feature against baseline).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0052","card":"q-mlops-014-automated-data-validation","sec":"mlops-s015","q":"Why compare correlations between features with a baseline?","a":"<p>A change in feature-feature correlation points to a change in multicollinearity, and a change in feature-target correlation means the relationship the model learned may no longer hold. Either can degrade the model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0053","card":"q-mlops-014-automated-data-validation","sec":"mlops-s015","q":"What is an out-of-distribution check for categorical and for continuous features?","a":"<p>For continuous features, flag values outside the quartile-based range seen in training (outliers). For categorical features, flag class values that never appeared in training.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0054","card":"q-mlops-014-automated-data-validation","sec":"mlops-s015","q":"What does a distribution check protect against?","a":"<p>It verifies the new data comes from the same population as the training data. If the mean, spread or class balance has shifted, the model's learned patterns may not apply, which is the data drift problem.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0055","card":"q-mlops-015-managed-feature-stores","sec":"mlops-s016","q":"What is a feature store and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>A central store of pre-processed features ready for ML, shared by several teams and models. It stops teams from recomputing the same features, saves cost and gives a consistent source of features for training.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0056","card":"q-mlops-015-managed-feature-stores","sec":"mlops-s016","q":"Why should feature store schemas be flexible?","a":"<p>So new features can be added without breaking existing consumers. A rigid schema would force every consumer to change when anyone adds a feature.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0057","card":"q-mlops-015-managed-feature-stores","sec":"mlops-s016","q":"Why keep related datasets separate in a feature store and join them at query time?","a":"<p>Separate datasets can evolve and be owned independently. Consumers combine them using linking attributes, so each team gets what it needs without changing the stored data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0058","card":"q-mlops-015-managed-feature-stores","sec":"mlops-s016","q":"Why must a feature store keep a registry of features?","a":"<p>The registry shows which features exist, so teams find and reuse them instead of processing the same data again.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0059","card":"q-mlops-015-managed-feature-stores","sec":"mlops-s016","q":"How should teams customize data from a shared feature store?","a":"<p>At query time, from a common stored format, not by changing the store. This avoids a stream of last-minute customization requests that would destabilize the shared store.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0060","card":"q-mlops-016-data-versioning-e-g-dvc","sec":"mlops-s017","q":"What is data versioning and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>It is version control for datasets, creating immutable baselines of raw, intermediate or feature data. It solves the problem that data changes over time, so a model could not be traced to or rebuilt from the exact data it used.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0061","card":"q-mlops-016-data-versioning-e-g-dvc","sec":"mlops-s017","q":"How does data versioning enable reproducibility?","a":"<p>A model's experiment links to a specific data version. Even after the data has changed, you can retrieve the old version and retrain to recreate the model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0062","card":"q-mlops-016-data-versioning-e-g-dvc","sec":"mlops-s017","q":"Why does data versioning let data scientists and data engineers work in parallel?","a":"<p>Scientists train on a fixed data version while engineers keep evolving the feature store. Data evolution is decoupled from model development.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0063","card":"q-mlops-016-data-versioning-e-g-dvc","sec":"mlops-s017","q":"Why is data versioning useful for rollback?","a":"<p>After bad processing corrupts the data, you can return to the last known good version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0064","card":"q-mlops-016-data-versioning-e-g-dvc","sec":"mlops-s017","q":"In DVC, why do you use <code>dvc add</code> and then commit to Git instead of committing the data file to Git?","a":"<p>Git handles large data files poorly. <code>dvc add data/train.csv</code> stores the file contents in DVC's cache (and a remote with <code>dvc push</code>) and writes a small <code>train.csv.dvc</code> metadata file containing a hash. You commit that small file to Git, and Git history then tracks which data version goes with which code version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0065","card":"q-mlops-016-data-versioning-e-g-dvc","sec":"mlops-s017","q":"How do you restore the data that matches an older code commit when using DVC?","a":"<p>Check out the Git commit or tag, then run <code>dvc checkout</code> (or <code>dvc pull</code> if the data is only in the remote). DVC reads the <code>.dvc</code> files from that commit and restores the matching data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0066","card":"q-mlops-016-data-versioning-e-g-dvc","sec":"mlops-s017","q":"DVC versus lakeFS: how do they differ?","a":"<p>DVC works alongside Git, tracking data through small metafiles and a cache or remote. lakeFS provides Git-like branches and commits directly on top of object storage, so data stays in place and many consumers can use branches. Choose DVC for Git-centered project workflows, and lakeFS for large shared data lakes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0067","card":"q-mlops-017-data-governance","sec":"mlops-s018","q":"What is data governance and what does it cover in an ML pipeline?","a":"<p>People, policies and processes that ensure integrity, security and usability of data. It covers all data (raw, intermediate, temporary, features, metadata), at rest and in motion.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0068","card":"q-mlops-017-data-governance","sec":"mlops-s018","q":"What elements make up data governance?","a":"<p>Consistency, integrity, security, privacy, resiliency and life cycle management.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0069","card":"q-mlops-017-data-governance","sec":"mlops-s018","q":"How do you handle privacy for ML training data?","a":"<p>Protect sensitive personal information, comply with laws and standards (for example GDPR), and use redaction and obfuscation inside the pipeline so raw personal data never reaches training or the feature store.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0070","card":"q-mlops-017-data-governance","sec":"mlops-s018","q":"Why is data resiliency part of governance?","a":"<p>Data loss or corruption stops training and breaks reproducibility. Backups, standby copies and redundancy, with recovery to the last known good state, protect against it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0071","card":"q-mlops-017-data-governance","sec":"mlops-s018","q":"What are the steps to implement data governance for ML?","a":"<p>Identify a governance team (owners and custodians), reuse existing production data policies, identify all data elements in the pipeline, classify them by security and privacy needs, and enforce the rules through automation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0072","card":"q-mlops-017-data-governance","sec":"mlops-s018","q":"Why enforce governance through automation instead of manual review?","a":"<p>Automation scales with data volume and cuts human error. Policies are applied the same way every time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0073","card":"q-mlops-017-data-governance","sec":"mlops-s018","q":"What does \"protection from adversarial attacks\" mean for training data?","a":"<p>Attackers may try to tamper with training data (poisoning) to change model behavior. Security controls on who can create or modify data, plus integrity checks, reduce that risk.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0074","card":"q-mlops-018-tools-for-data-processing","sec":"mlops-s019","q":"Apache Spark versus Apache Kafka in a data pipeline: what role does each play?","a":"<p>Spark is a distributed engine for large-scale batch (and stream) processing and transformation. Kafka is a distributed event log for ingesting and moving streaming data between systems. They are often combined: Kafka carries events and Spark processes them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0075","card":"q-mlops-018-tools-for-data-processing","sec":"mlops-s019","q":"When would you choose a NoSQL database like MongoDB or Cassandra over MySQL for ML data?","a":"<p>Choose NoSQL for flexible or evolving schemas, semi-structured data and horizontal scale. Choose a relational database such as MySQL for structured data with fixed schema and transactional consistency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0076","card":"q-mlops-019-managed-training-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s020","q":"Describe the flow of a managed training pipeline.","a":"<p>Take data from the feature store, set hyperparameters, plan the experiment, train, validate against performance goals, test on an independent test set, review results, and if the goal is not met update parameters and retrain.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0077","card":"q-mlops-019-managed-training-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s020","q":"Why use separate environments for training?","a":"<p>Dev and test environments are used for experimenting. The final training run happens in a controlled environment so the production model is built in a clean, repeatable way.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0078","card":"q-mlops-019-managed-training-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s020","q":"What can be automated in a training pipeline?","a":"<p>Hyperparameter tuning (for example grid search), triggering training when new data arrives, and comparing the new model against the baseline model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0079","card":"q-mlops-019-managed-training-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s020","q":"Why must you test a model on an independent test set after validation?","a":"<p>Hyperparameters are tuned against the validation results, so validation performance is optimistically biased. A held-out test set the model never influenced gives an unbiased estimate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0080","card":"q-mlops-020-creating-data-labels","sec":"mlops-s021","q":"What is data labeling and why is it needed?","a":"<p>Labeling (annotation) adds contextual tags that act as the target for supervised learning. Unstructured data such as text, images and media often has no labels, available labels may be inaccurate or incomplete, and one dataset may need different labels for different use cases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0081","card":"q-mlops-020-creating-data-labels","sec":"mlops-s021","q":"Compare the ways to label data: domain experts, crowdsourcing, professional annotators and programmatic labeling.","a":"<p>Domain experts: low scale, high accuracy, high cost. Crowdsourcing: high scale, variable accuracy (knowledge and bias), low cost. Professional annotators: medium scale, high accuracy, high cost. Programmatic labeling: massive scale, accuracy depends on the labeling model, low cost once built.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0082","card":"q-mlops-020-creating-data-labels","sec":"mlops-s021","q":"What is a recommended labeling strategy for a large dataset?","a":"<p>Combine methods. Have experts or professionals label a base dataset, then use it to train volunteers or labeling programs to label a much larger corpus. Check quality with samples reviewed by experts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0083","card":"q-mlops-020-creating-data-labels","sec":"mlops-s021","q":"What is the drawback of crowdsourced labels?","a":"<p>Accuracy varies with the workers' knowledge and bias. Mitigate it with clear guidelines, several workers per item with agreement checks, and expert review of samples.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0084","card":"q-mlops-020-creating-data-labels","sec":"mlops-s021","q":"What is programmatic labeling and what is its trade-off?","a":"<p>Labels are assigned by code or models (rules, heuristics, weak supervision, a model) instead of people. It labels massive datasets cheaply, but takes time to build and its labels are only as accurate as the rules or model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0085","card":"q-mlops-021-experiment-tracking","sec":"mlops-s022","q":"What should you track for each ML experiment?","a":"<p>Model setup (algorithm, architecture, hyperparameters), inputs (dataset and version, train/validation/test splits), outputs (serialized model, metrics such as accuracy, error and F1), and analysis (comparison with earlier runs, findings, next steps).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0086","card":"q-mlops-021-experiment-tracking","sec":"mlops-s022","q":"Why track experiments with a tool instead of a spreadsheet?","a":"<p>Manual tracking is slow and error-prone. A tool integrated into the training pipeline records parameters, data version and metrics automatically, so any run can be compared and reproduced.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0087","card":"q-mlops-021-experiment-tracking","sec":"mlops-s022","q":"How does experiment tracking help decide whether to promote a model?","a":"<p>It shows the effect of parameter and data changes and progress toward requirements. It also allows automated promotion rules based on tracked metrics.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0088","card":"q-mlops-021-experiment-tracking","sec":"mlops-s022","q":"In MLflow tracking, what do <code>log_param</code> and <code>log_metric</code> record, and why separate them?","a":"<p>In a run opened with <code>mlflow.start_run()</code>, <code>mlflow.log_param(\"lr\", 0.01)</code> records an input setting (a configuration value fixed for the run). <code>mlflow.log_metric(\"accuracy\", 0.93)</code> records a result and can be logged repeatedly with a step to chart progress. The separation lets the UI compare configurations and plot outcomes.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import mlflow\n\nwith mlflow.start_run():\n    mlflow.log_param(&quot;learning_rate&quot;, 0.01)\n    mlflow.log_metric(&quot;accuracy&quot;, 0.93)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0089","card":"q-mlops-021-experiment-tracking","sec":"mlops-s022","q":"What does it mean that an experiment is reproducible if configuration and inputs are tracked?","a":"<p>With the algorithm, hyperparameters, data version and splits recorded, you can rerun the same code and get the same model. Set random seeds as well, because randomness can otherwise change results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0090","card":"q-mlops-022-automl","sec":"mlops-s023","q":"What is AutoML and where does it help in the ML life cycle?","a":"<p>Automation of ML activities with little human intervention. It helps in feature engineering (detect issues, check feature-target correlation, select features), training (choose algorithm, tune hyperparameters, build ensembles), deployment decisions (judge production readiness, retrain on drift or bias) and custom models (adapt a base model per customer at scale).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0091","card":"q-mlops-022-automl","sec":"mlops-s023","q":"What are the benefits and shortcomings of AutoML?","a":"<p>Benefits: automated retraining, faster training and deployment, better results through search, no need for data scientists to operate it, and custom models at scale. Shortcomings: errors if the automation is not validated, issues from new data or feature relationships, untracked bias, capacity problems with multi-round grid search, and weaker explainability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0092","card":"q-mlops-022-automl","sec":"mlops-s023","q":"What should you do before adopting AutoML?","a":"<p>Achieve manual repeatability first. Then pick pipeline tools, use experiment tracking, add automated sanity tests on outputs, monitor production for degradation and review regularly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0093","card":"q-mlops-022-automl","sec":"mlops-s023","q":"When is AutoML a good fit and when is it risky?","a":"<p>A good fit for many similar models (per-customer models) and for teams without deep ML expertise. It is risky where explainability, bias control or strict validation is required, since automation can hide those problems.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0094","card":"q-mlops-022-automl","sec":"mlops-s023","q":"Why does grid search become a capacity problem at scale?","a":"<p>Grid search tries every combination of the listed hyperparameter values, so the number of runs multiplies with each added parameter. Multi-round searches across many models can exhaust compute budgets. Random search or Bayesian optimization are common alternatives.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0095","card":"q-mlops-023-training-with-generative-ai","sec":"mlops-s024","q":"Compare pre-training and fine-tuning of a foundation model.","a":"<p>Pre-training creates a foundation model from scratch on general-purpose data and learns general language patterns and knowledge. It has billions of parameters, very high cost and long duration, and is done by few organizations. Fine-tuning customizes an existing foundation model with domain or task data. It learns task-specific patterns on top, costs far less and is what most enterprises do.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0096","card":"q-mlops-023-training-with-generative-ai","sec":"mlops-s024","q":"Why do most enterprises fine-tune instead of pre-train a model?","a":"<p>Pre-training needs huge data, compute and time. Fine-tuning reuses what a foundation model has already learned and adapts it to the task at much lower cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0097","card":"q-mlops-023-training-with-generative-ai","sec":"mlops-s024","q":"Do MLOps principles still apply when training generative AI models?","a":"<p>Yes. Versioning of data and models, experiment tracking, reproducibility and automation still matter. Only the scale, data type and cost change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0098","card":"q-mlops-024-tools-for-training","sec":"mlops-s025","q":"What roles do Git, Jupyter and MLflow play in a training workflow?","a":"<p>Git versions code and notebooks. Jupyter is the interactive environment for developing models. MLflow (or Kubeflow, Weights &amp; Biases) tracks experiments, parameters and metrics.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0099","card":"q-mlops-024-tools-for-training","sec":"mlops-s025","q":"Kubeflow versus MLflow: how do they differ?","a":"<p>Kubeflow is a Kubernetes-based platform for running ML pipelines and workflows at scale. MLflow is a lighter tool focused on experiment tracking, model packaging and a model registry. Choose Kubeflow when you run on Kubernetes and need orchestration, and MLflow for tracking and registry with less infrastructure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0100","card":"q-mlops-025-model-versioning","sec":"mlops-s026","q":"Why version models and what should a version link to?","a":"<p>Versions establish lineage and support life cycle management. A model version links to the data version and code version that produced it, so you can find its inputs and hyperparameters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0101","card":"q-mlops-025-model-versioning","sec":"mlops-s026","q":"Why do integration and testing teams need stable model version references?","a":"<p>They test against a fixed model. If the model changed during testing, results would not be comparable. A version gives a stable reference until integration and testing complete.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0102","card":"q-mlops-025-model-versioning","sec":"mlops-s026","q":"How should you design a model version numbering scheme?","a":"<p>Make it resemble software versioning (for example major and minor numbers). The exact scheme is organization-specific, as long as it is consistent and tied to the linked baselines.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0103","card":"q-mlops-025-model-versioning","sec":"mlops-s026","q":"What does a model version let you track over time?","a":"<p>Performance across versions, so you can see whether a new version improves on earlier ones.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0104","card":"q-mlops-026-model-registry","sec":"mlops-s027","q":"What is a model registry and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>A central repository that stores models and their metadata in a queryable database, with status changes and audit trails. It solves scattered model files with unknown lineage. It does for models what a feature store does for data and a code repository does for code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0105","card":"q-mlops-026-model-registry","sec":"mlops-s027","q":"What should a model registry store for each model?","a":"<p>The serialized model artifact, a unique ID, version and description, the hyperparameters used, the current life cycle status, an audit trail (timestamps of state changes, test results, deployments) and lineage links (data version, code or notebook version, experiment results).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0106","card":"q-mlops-026-model-registry","sec":"mlops-s027","q":"What are the MLOps benefits of a model registry?","a":"<p>Traceability, life cycle tracking, collaboration across teams and time, automation of model creation and consumption, and governance through access control and auditing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0107","card":"q-mlops-026-model-registry","sec":"mlops-s027","q":"Why should there be one central model registry shared by all ML teams?","a":"<p>It gives one place to find models and their status, avoids duplicated work, and lets governance apply the same access and audit rules everywhere.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0108","card":"q-mlops-026-model-registry","sec":"mlops-s027","q":"How does the MLflow Model Registry label which version is production?","a":"<p>In MLflow 2.9 and later, model version aliases (for example a <code>champion</code> alias) and tags are the recommended mechanism. Older MLflow used fixed stages (Staging, Production, Archived), which are deprecated. Check the docs of your version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0109","card":"q-mlops-027-benchmarking-models","sec":"mlops-s028","q":"What is model benchmarking and when does it happen?","a":"<p>Comparing a model and its versions against baselines and competing models, after it passes its fitness test and before integration. It runs in an environment close to production and measures performance and operational metrics.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0110","card":"q-mlops-027-benchmarking-models","sec":"mlops-s028","q":"Why benchmark in a production-like environment instead of in a notebook?","a":"<p>A model that looks good in development may need more compute or add latency under realistic hardware and load. A production-like test shows whether it meets operational requirements.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0111","card":"q-mlops-027-benchmarking-models","sec":"mlops-s028","q":"What does a benchmarking setup need?","a":"<p>Production-like hardware, an isolated environment, a test harness with the same dataset and expected results for each model, test executors with required pacing and capacity, a tracking platform to log predictions, latency, errors and resource use, and a mode: both models at once or one against a stored baseline.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0112","card":"q-mlops-027-benchmarking-models","sec":"mlops-s028","q":"What metrics do you collect in benchmarking?","a":"<p>Performance metrics (accuracy, F1, precision, recall) and operational metrics (latency, scaling, resource utilization).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0113","card":"q-mlops-027-benchmarking-models","sec":"mlops-s028","q":"What are best practices for benchmarking?","a":"<p>Use apples-to-apples conditions (same data, hardware, load), log everything at the finest granularity, automate and integrate it in pipelines, and use experiment tracking tools for long-term analysis.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0114","card":"q-mlops-027-benchmarking-models","sec":"mlops-s028","q":"Why isolate the benchmarking environment from other activity?","a":"<p>Other workloads on shared hardware or network add noise to latency and resource measurements, which makes comparisons unreliable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0115","card":"q-mlops-028-model-life-cycle-management","sec":"mlops-s029","q":"What states does a model go through in a typical life cycle, and what triggers each transition?","a":"<p>Created (training produced it), then benchmarking: Passed or Discarded. Passed models go to integration testing: Approved or Archived. Approved models are Deployed, and later Retired when a new version replaces them. The states are a sample and each organization customizes them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0116","card":"q-mlops-028-model-life-cycle-management","sec":"mlops-s029","q":"How should promotion criteria for models be defined?","a":"<p>Define the life cycle and criteria during requirements, base them on the performance and operational metrics in the requirements, and include non-ML criteria (such as total issues found) for integration testing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0117","card":"q-mlops-028-model-life-cycle-management","sec":"mlops-s029","q":"Should model promotion be automated?","a":"<p>Automate promotion on metric thresholds, but keep human oversight at first and reduce it as the pipeline matures. Record every decision and history in the model registry.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0118","card":"q-mlops-028-model-life-cycle-management","sec":"mlops-s029","q":"What is the difference between a Discarded and an Archived model?","a":"<p>Discarded models failed benchmarking and are not used further. Archived models passed benchmarking but failed integration testing, so they are kept on record, for example for later analysis.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0119","card":"q-mlops-029-tools-for-model-management","sec":"mlops-s030","q":"Why keep the number of model management tools low?","a":"<p>Each tool needs administration, integration and maintenance. More tools means more overhead and more integration points that can fail.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0120","card":"q-mlops-029-tools-for-model-management","sec":"mlops-s030","q":"What do MLPerf and DAWNBench provide?","a":"<p>They are standard benchmarks for comparing ML training and inference performance, useful for comparing hardware and systems. Use them for system-level comparison, and your own benchmark data for model-level acceptance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0121","card":"q-mlops-030-solution-integration-pipeline","sec":"mlops-s031","q":"Why does a model need an integration pipeline?","a":"<p>A model alone is not a solution. It must be embedded in other code such as APIs, UIs, databases and microservices, and tested together.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0122","card":"q-mlops-030-solution-integration-pipeline","sec":"mlops-s031","q":"Describe the stages of a solution integration pipeline.","a":"<p>Train the model and store it in the model registry. Convert notebook code to software, then regression test it to get an ML service. In parallel non-ML code evolves. Then run integration testing, performance testing, and package and deliver the model executable with the non-ML code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0123","card":"q-mlops-030-solution-integration-pipeline","sec":"mlops-s031","q":"Why does the model registry sit between training and integration?","a":"<p>Integration pulls a specific approved, versioned model from the registry. That gives traceability and avoids integrating files copied by hand.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0124","card":"q-mlops-031-notebook-to-software","sec":"mlops-s032","q":"Why must notebook code be converted before a model can be deployed?","a":"<p>Notebook code is interactive and exploratory. Deployment needs an executable, testable, versioned module with defined interfaces that reproduces the original notebook processing and performance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0125","card":"q-mlops-031-notebook-to-software","sec":"mlops-s032","q":"What forms can a deployed model take?","a":"<p>Embedded in a non-ML executable (function, SDK, Python package, serverless function). A batch service (a job reading from a database and writing predictions back). A request-response service (REST API). A streaming service (reads from a queue, writes to a queue).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0126","card":"q-mlops-031-notebook-to-software","sec":"mlops-s032","q":"What parts of notebook code must be converted into the production module?","a":"<p>Pre-processing (tokenization, encoding, scaling), the model file from the registry, inference code, post-processing (decoding, reverse scaling, mapping to business outcomes), input and output interfaces, and build, test and deploy infrastructure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0127","card":"q-mlops-031-notebook-to-software","sec":"mlops-s032","q":"How do you verify a notebook-to-software conversion is correct?","a":"<p>Run a regression test. Use the benchmark input data and results from the original model as the baseline, and verify the converted service gives the same performance. Focus on pre- and post-processing, where differences usually appear.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0128","card":"q-mlops-031-notebook-to-software","sec":"mlops-s032","q":"Why is it risky to reimplement pre-processing separately in the service?","a":"<p>If the service transforms inputs differently from training (different scaling, encoding or tokenizer), predictions silently degrade. This is called training-serving skew. Reuse the same code or fitted transformers, and regression test.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0129","card":"q-mlops-031-notebook-to-software","sec":"mlops-s032","q":"Should the model file be packaged with the service or loaded at runtime?","a":"<p>Both are valid. Packaging gives a self-contained, versioned deployable. Loading from the registry or external repository at runtime lets you update the model without rebuilding the service, but adds a runtime dependency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0130","card":"q-mlops-031-notebook-to-software","sec":"mlops-s032","q":"How do you choose between batch, REST and streaming deployment of a model?","a":"<p>Choose by how predictions are consumed. Batch when results are needed periodically and latency is not critical. REST when an application needs a prediction per request immediately. Streaming when events flow continuously through queues. Embedded when the application must run offline or with no network call.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0131","card":"q-mlops-032-integration-patterns","sec":"mlops-s033","q":"ML and non-ML code evolve at the same time. What are the two integration options?","a":"<p>Option 1: hold the ML version constant through the whole non-ML release and introduce the new model in the next release. Option 2: adapt the new ML version as soon as it exists, even mid-cycle.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0132","card":"q-mlops-032-integration-patterns","sec":"mlops-s033","q":"Compare holding the ML version constant with adopting the new model as soon as possible.","a":"<p>Holding constant: stable development, simpler collaboration and easy automation, but slower time to market (two releases) and later issue detection. Adopting early: faster overall and earlier issue detection, but mid-cycle changes may cause problems, the teams must coordinate closely, and automatic pulling of new versions may break the pipeline.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0133","card":"q-mlops-032-integration-patterns","sec":"mlops-s033","q":"How do you choose between the two integration patterns?","a":"<p>Decide by requirements, such as the need for speed versus stability, and mitigate the weaknesses of the chosen pattern with process, for example close coordination for the early option.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0134","card":"q-mlops-033-best-practices-for-integration","sec":"mlops-s034","q":"What are the best practices for integrating ML and non-ML code?","a":"<p>Define code promotion policies for each stage, define measurable acceptance criteria (pass/fail tests or metrics), automate integration and testing, run both ML and non-ML test cases, and keep both teams informed of each other's work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0135","card":"q-mlops-033-best-practices-for-integration","sec":"mlops-s034","q":"Why automate integration testing in ML projects?","a":"<p>Both the model and the application code change often. Manual testing cannot keep up and misses regressions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0136","card":"q-mlops-033-best-practices-for-integration","sec":"mlops-s034","q":"Why do acceptance criteria need to be measurable?","a":"<p>A measurable pass or fail rule lets the pipeline decide promotion automatically and avoids opinion-based decisions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0137","card":"q-mlops-034-integration-with-generative-ai","sec":"mlops-s035","q":"In a generative AI application, what role do prompts play in integration and how should you test them?","a":"<p>Prompts are the integration code between the application and the model. Test them within the application, and cover them thoroughly if users can change them, because small wording changes can alter model behavior.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0138","card":"q-mlops-034-integration-with-generative-ai","sec":"mlops-s035","q":"What should be tested when integrating a generative AI app with a model service?","a":"<p>Model integration (compute, bandwidth, security), prompts, vector databases in RAG apps, AI agent tools and their external integration points, guardrails (privacy, hallucination and harmful-content filtering), and performance (latency and cost).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0139","card":"q-mlops-034-integration-with-generative-ai","sec":"mlops-s035","q":"Why do most teams consume generative models through a cloud service?","a":"<p>These models are very large, and need expensive hardware to run. Teams call a hosted API such as OpenAI's, or host an open-source model on a cloud such as AWS or GCP, so they integrate with a service instead of running the model locally.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0140","card":"q-mlops-034-integration-with-generative-ai","sec":"mlops-s035","q":"Why test guardrails in the integrated application and not only in isolation?","a":"<p>Guardrails must work in production with real prompts, data and traffic. Integration tests check that privacy filtering and harmful-content blocking actually apply along the full request path.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0141","card":"q-mlops-034-integration-with-generative-ai","sec":"mlops-s035","q":"Why track latency and cost during integration of a generative AI app?","a":"<p>Both affect user experience and budget. Large models are slower and billed per token, so a prompt design that works functionally may still be too slow or expensive.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0142","card":"q-mlops-035-8-tools-cheat-sheet-principles","sec":"mlops-s036","q":"Which tool categories does an ML build pipeline need, and name one example of each.","a":"<p>Data processing (Apache Spark), databases (MongoDB), data versioning (DVC), model development (Python with Jupyter and Git), experiment management (MLflow), model registry (MLflow), benchmarking (MLPerf) and AutoML (Databricks, Kubeflow). Several tools cover more than one category, which helps keep the tool count low.</p>","hand":true}];
